#  NOT YET COVERED
#
#  Every entry above is clinical or biological: propionic acidemia, PCC
#  enzymology, carnitine handling, newborn screening, and the mRNA trial.
#  Twenty sources, and five of them are cited in the main text.
#
#  The paper's own contribution is an identifiability result and an
#  experimental design, and it currently cites nothing from either
#  literature. A reviewer's first question is how this relates to
#  existing work, and the reference list has no answer to it.
#
#  The areas that need covering are listed below as topics, not as
#  entries. Each one has to be resolved to a DOI or a PMID by hand and
#  then fetched like everything else here. No identifier is written from
#  memory, which is the whole point of this file, so none is guessed at
#  here either.
#
#    - structural identifiability of nonlinear ODE models, and a
#      citation for StructuralIdentifiability.jl, which Section 2.6
#      already reports results from and does not cite
#    - practical identifiability and profile likelihood, and the
#      distinction between structural and practical non-identifiability,
#      which is the distinction Section 3 turns on
#    - Fisher information, sloppy models and sensitivity spectra, which
#      is the established language for the obstruction in Section 3
#    - optimal experimental design for dynamic systems, and input design
#      in particular, which is what Section 4 does
#    - quasi-steady-state approximation and bounds on model reduction
#      error, which is the open question Section 7 asks the reader
#    - identifiability in pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic models,
#      the nearest applied literature to this design
# =====================================================================

# =====================================================================
#  Registered September 2026, after network egress became available in
#  the working container. Every identifier below was resolved against
#  CrossRef or NCBI and the returned author, journal, volume and pages
#  were checked against the citation. refs/literature_pending.md is
#  discharged by this block; see audit/PHASE6_REVIEWS_SEP2026.md.
#
#  NOT registered, deliberately: van Noort et al. 2024,
#  doi:10.1002/psp4.13148. Resolved and read; it is a workflow review of
#  parameter-identifiability methods and uses "aliasing" for parameters
#  with similar sensitivity profiles, NOT for sampling synchronous with
#  dosing. It is not support for the dose-phase result. It is kept out of
#  refs.bib so that it cannot be cited here by accident.
# =====================================================================

# --- clinical, from the September 2026 reviews -----------------------
# European survey, 186 PA patients / 47 centres / 14 countries. 74% were
# PRESCRIBED natural protein below WHO/FAO/UNU safe levels; PFAA supplied a
# median 44% (14-83%) of total protein; 63% tube fed. NOTE: this is what
# patients were prescribed, not what they ate. No PA study reports repeated
# as-eaten intake, which is why the recording error in R17 is unbounded.
pa_diet_survey          pmid:29021961

# 16 children with PKU, 12-18 one-day diaries and ~22 blood spots each over
# 6 months. Median prescribed natural protein 6 g/day against an actual
# 10 g/day: actual = 167% of prescribed, a median +40% (P < 0.001), range
# -2.5 to +11.5 g/day. The only prescription-vs-actual measurement in any
# protein-restricted inborn error, and the footing for R17's 40%.
pku_prescribed_actual   doi:10.3390/nu15234903

# PCC partially purified from cultured fibroblasts. "Each of seven lines
# from patients with propionic acidemia had clearly detectable PCC activity
# which was less than 5% of that in control lines." NOTE: fibroblast
# enzymology, not in vivo hepatic residual capacity. Bears on open question
# (b), 5% vs 10%, without settling it.
pcc_residual_activity   pmid:481943

# The original 1-13C-propionate breath test: 5 PA, 8 MMA, 1 MCD, 1 TC-II,
# 5 controls; IV or oral bolus, 3 h collection. Recovery and half-time in
# PA significantly below normal, and "well correlated with clinical severity
# in PA, but did not correlate in MMA". Reports NO repeatability CV, which
# is the gap Tier 1 fills. Also: "differences between MMA and PA may
# indicate different distribution of propionate pools".
breath_test_original    pmid:1909779

# --- methods: identifiability and estimation -------------------------
# Profile likelihood for partially observed dynamical models, and the
# structural-vs-practical distinction the flat K_m profile turns on.
profile_likelihood      doi:10.1093/bioinformatics/btp358

# Relative curvature measures of nonlinearity. The classical statement of
# what R14 retracted: the eps^4 is the curvature of an exact fibre against
# its own tangent.
curvature_measures      doi:10.1111/j.2517-6161.1980.tb01094.x

# The boundary LRT mixture. Cited for why the correction must NOT be applied
# here: the mixture is for a parameter being TESTED on a boundary, and here
# that parameter is a nuisance.
boundary_lrt            doi:10.1080/01621459.1987.10478472

# Bootstrap inconsistency on a boundary. Explains the measured bootstrap
# failures: percentile 0.77-0.82, parametric at fitted nuisances 0.683.
boundary_bootstrap      doi:10.1111/1468-0262.00114

# Population vs individual identifiability in mixed-effects models.
# Supports "describes the prior's population, not one patient".
nlme_identifiability    doi:10.1007/s10928-015-9459-4

# Structural identifiability of the standard PD model families. The nearest
# prior art to Phase 5 steps 3 and 4.
pd_identifiability      doi:10.3389/fphys.2016.00590

# Variable projection, which is exactly i1_estimator.py's estimator.
varpro_review           doi:10.1088/0266-5611/19/2/201
varpro_founding         doi:10.1137/0710036

# --- methods: experiment and input design ----------------------------
# Joint optimal design on dose AND sample times: the design counterpart of
# the phase-spread schedule. Nyberg, Karlsson and Hooker, JPKPD 36(2):125.
# NOTE: often miscited to Duffull; the registrar settles it.
design_dose_and_times   doi:10.1007/s10928-009-9114-z

# The qualitative design step: choosing an input/output configuration so
# that parameters are identifiable at all. The general form of the
# dose-phase result.
qualitative_design      doi:10.1016/0005-1098(90)90116-Y

# Optimal input signals for parameter estimation. IEEE TAC 19(6):753-768,
# confirmed against the registrar: often miscited as "Vol. 29" or
# "AC-21, 55-64".
